Random forest & 200 trees, balanced class weights, same folds \\
Cohort & one record per patient, drawn once with a fixed random state \\
Analyses & $K$ sweep with one $k$-means fit per $K$. Imbalance analysis with one subsample and one $k$-means fit \\
MIT-BIH & at most 400 beats per record and class, then 1\,500 per class drawn at random, non-beat annotations excluded \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Federated clustering simulation}

To probe deployment under data siloing we used federated $k$-means, with centroid aggregation in the manner of federated averaging (FedAvg) \cite{fedavg2017}. The 2\,542 records were assigned by patient to ten simulated sites. In the independent and identically distributed (IID) setting each patient was assigned to a site at random. In the non-IID setting the patients of each class were split across the sites by proportions drawn from a Dirichlet distribution with concentration 0.3. The sites then differ sharply in arrhythmia prevalence. Sites with fewer than two records were dropped. In each of 15 rounds every site ran three local iterations of Lloyd's algorithm from the shared centroids. Each iteration assigns the site's records to their nearest centroid and recomputes each centroid as the mean of its records. The server then averaged the site centroids, weighted by their cluster counts. Each of ten seeds drew a new partition and a new initialisation. Two steps used the pooled records: the power transform was fitted on all records, and the initial centroids were drawn from all records. The heterogeneity is therefore a simulated class-proportion skew across ten partitions of one dataset. We report the ARI of the final global centroids beside centralized $k$-means with ten initialisations on the same conditioned representation.

\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Clustering by representation and algorithm}

In the PTB-XL benchmark the choice of representation changed the ARI more than the choice of clustering algorithm (Table~\ref{tab:main}, Fig.~\ref{fig:heatmap}). Across the four clustering algorithms given the number of classes, the ARI of the hand-crafted features ranged from 0.544 to 0.711 and that of ECGFounder from 0.709 to 0.885. Across representations, the $k$-means ARI ranged from 0.000 to 0.885. ECGFounder clustered best (ARI up to 0.885 with $k$-means), and the hand-crafted features reached 0.711 with Ward. HuBERT-ECG reached at most 0.195, so the two foundation models did not behave as one category. Raw flattening and the conv-AE scored at chance (ARI at most 0.013).

