\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\linewidth]{figures/fig1_ari_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{PTB-XL SR-versus-AF clustering ARI by representation and clustering algorithm under the power transform, on 2\,542 records (1\,282 SR, 1\,260 AF). Each $k$-means, spectral and GMM cell is the mean over ten seeds, and each Ward and HDBSCAN cell is one deterministic fit. The seed spread is given in Table~\ref{tab:main}. The axis labels kmean, agglo, spect, gmm and hdbsc stand for $k$-means, Ward, spectral clustering, the GMM and HDBSCAN, and raw-flatten stands for raw flattening.}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure}

The ranking of the representations did not come from the initialisation of the clustering algorithms. Across ten seeds the $k$-means solutions were stable (Table~\ref{tab:main}). A paired Wilcoxon signed-rank test on the per-seed $k$-means ARI compared the representations adjacent in the ranking of ECGFounder, the hand-crafted features, HuBERT-ECG and raw flattening. Each of the three contrasts gave $p = 0.002$, the floor for ten paired samples, so the test fixes the direction of each contrast, and its size is read from the difference in mean ARI. On the chance-corrected scale of the ARI, 0 is the chance level and 1 means identical partitions. On this scale ECGFounder exceeded the hand-crafted features by 0.242, the hand-crafted features exceeded HuBERT-ECG by 0.470, and HuBERT-ECG exceeded raw flattening by 0.174.

Two further checks agreed with the ARI ranking. The hand-crafted $k$-means partition had a purity of 0.901, against 0.506 for raw flattening, whose purity is close to the 0.504 that assigning every record to the majority class gives. Purity therefore separated the two representations only when read against the majority-class proportion. With the AMI for $K$ from 2 to 8, ECGFounder led at every $K$ (0.83 falling to 0.45), and the hand-crafted features followed (0.56 to 0.33). HuBERT-ECG and raw flattening stayed low (below 0.14 and near 0), so fixing the cluster count to the number of classes did not flatter the comparison.

HDBSCAN and spectral clustering gave low scores for most representations, each for reasons of its own. HDBSCAN labelled every record as noise for raw flattening, the conv-AE and HuBERT-ECG, so these solutions form one cluster and score an ARI of 0. For the hand-crafted features and ECGFounder its largest cluster held 0.140 and 0.202 of the records, far less than the half of the records that each class holds. HDBSCAN therefore split each class across several clusters or into noise, which kept its ARI at 0.137 or below. A possible cause of the low spectral scores, such as 0.016 for HuBERT-ECG, is a poorly connected nearest-neighbour affinity graph, which we did not check. We report both algorithms as fitted, without tuning them.

\subsection{Separability and clusterability}\label{sec:sep}

The supervised ceilings (last column of Table~\ref{tab:main}, Fig.~\ref{fig:sep}) expose the gap between separability and clusterability. The ceiling is an accuracy whose chance level is the majority-class proportion (0.504). The ARI is corrected for chance, with chance level 0. The two numbers are therefore read against their own chance levels and are not compared directly. Raw flattening carried almost no linearly decodable label information (ceiling 0.519), consistent with its chance-level clustering. The conv-AE was more separable (ceiling 0.678) yet clustered at chance (best ARI 0.008). HuBERT-ECG showed the largest gap between separability and clusterability, with a ceiling of 0.899 and a best ARI of 0.195. ECGFounder and the hand-crafted features achieved both high separability and high clusterability. We read the conv-AE and HuBERT-ECG as holding label information that does not lie along their dominant axes of variation. This reading is consistent with the gap between linear-probe accuracy and clustering quality reported for self-supervised image models \cite{sslclustering2025}. The random forest showed that label information can be present where clustering does not recover it: on raw flattening it reached an accuracy of 0.71, above the supervised ceiling of 0.519, while the clustering ARI was 0.

ECGFounder and the hand-crafted features both separated and clustered the classes, and we offer an explanation for each, which we did not test directly. ECGFounder was pretrained with supervision on 150 label categories that include rhythm descriptions \cite{arxiv_2410_04133}. Its feature space is therefore likely organised by diagnosis, which would also account for the alignment with the clinical label that Filice et al. found in its embedding \cite{lookingbeyond2026}. The hand-crafted features measure the criteria that define atrial fibrillation, irregular RR intervals and absent P waves \cite{Van_Gelder_2024}. The class difference is therefore likely to lie along the features' own axes, which is consistent with both their high ceiling (0.942) and their high ARI (0.711).

HuBERT-ECG's embedding held the SR-versus-AF label, as its ceiling of 0.899 shows, but we read that label as lying off the directions of largest variance. HuBERT-ECG was pretrained by self-supervision without diagnostic labels \cite{hubertecg2024}, and here it saw only the first 5\,s at 100\,Hz, averaged over time. The logistic regression of the ceiling can use a direction of small variance that separates the classes, whereas $k$-means and the GMM partition along the directions of largest variance. In this embedding those directions appear to follow structure other than the rhythm class, which agrees with the dataset-level organisation that Filice et al. found for HuBERT-ECG \cite{lookingbeyond2026}. Recovering the SR-versus-AF information from this embedding therefore needs labels in this setting. We did not test whether the shorter, unfiltered input adds to the gap.

Raw flattening and the conv-AE scored at chance for different reasons. With $k$-means, raw flattening placed on average 0.967 of the records in one cluster, close to a majority-class assignment. The conv-AE formed two clusters of similar size (the larger held 0.541 of the records at seed 0) that did not follow the rhythm class. Raw flattening compares samples at fixed positions without beat alignment, and the conv-AE is trained to reconstruct the waveform. We therefore read neither representation as placing the rhythm difference along its dominant axes, an interpretation we did not test.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/fig3_sep_vs_clust.pdf}
\caption{Separability and clusterability of the five PTB-XL representations, on the same 2\,542 records. Each point is one representation. The horizontal value is its supervised ceiling, the patient-grouped five-fold accuracy of a balanced logistic regression, with chance level 0.504. The vertical value is the mean ARI of its best clustering algorithm, a mean over ten seeds or one fit for Ward and HDBSCAN, with chance level 0. The dashed line, labelled parity, joins equal values on the two axes and is drawn for orientation. Each coordinate is read against its own chance level, 0.504 for the ceiling and 0 for the ARI. Table~\ref{tab:main} gives every clustering algorithm and the seed spread, and Fig.~\ref{fig:trap} shows one marker per fit. The point label raw-flatten stands for raw flattening.}
\label{fig:sep}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Silhouette and degenerate solutions}

In this benchmark the silhouette scored a degenerate one-cluster solution higher than a solution that recovered the classes. Raw flattening with Ward attained a silhouette of 0.81 while one cluster held 0.9996 of the records and the ARI was 0.000. The hand-crafted features, which recovered the classes (ARI 0.711, balanced clusters), had a \emph{lower} silhouette of 0.28 (Fig.~\ref{fig:trap}). A degenerate one-cluster solution maximises compactness while carrying no class information. This artefact lies behind the original project's silhouette of 0.89. Chance-corrected external metrics, read together with cluster-size checks, are needed to detect it.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/fig2_silhouette_trap.pdf}
\caption{Silhouette against ARI on the 2\,542 PTB-XL records under the power transform. Each marker is one clustering fit: ten seeds for $k$-means, spectral clustering and the GMM, and one fit for Ward and HDBSCAN. The horizontal axis is the internal silhouette score and the vertical axis the chance-corrected ARI. Marker size is the largest-cluster fraction. Fits without a silhouette value, such as an HDBSCAN solution with every record labelled as noise, are not drawn. The degenerate one-cluster solutions (large markers, bottom right) attain a high silhouette at zero ARI. The legend label raw-flatten stands for raw flattening.}
\label{fig:trap}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Effect of the conditioner}\label{sec:cond}

The choice of conditioner changed the outcome materially. For the hand-crafted features the mean ARI over all fits of the five clustering algorithms was 0.63 under standard scaling and 0.61 under the power transform, but fell to 0.36 under robust scaling and 0.23 under the quantile transform. The effect was not confined to the hand-crafted features: ECGFounder's mean ARI dropped from 0.87 under standard scaling to 0.09 under robust scaling. A possible cause, which we did not test, is that robust scaling leaves the heavy tails of the arrhythmic records unbounded, so that a few records dominate the Euclidean distance. The pilot analysis showed the matching outcome for the hand-crafted features, whose $k$-means partition collapsed under robust scaling (Section~\ref{sec:clust}).

\subsection{Generalization to MIT-BIH beat morphology}

The MIT-BIH beat task, with three representations, showed the same pattern as PTB-XL (Table~\ref{tab:mitbih}). On the N-versus-V task the 21 hand-crafted beat features clustered well (ARI up to 0.783). The raw beat window did not (ARI at most 0.049), although the beats are aligned on the R peak. The conv-AE again separated without clustering: its supervised ceiling was 0.927, but its ARI was at most 0.127.

