\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

Which representation clustered best depended on the class proportions in the settings tested here: two binary tasks, five clustering algorithms, two named foundation models and a simulated federation. On balanced PTB-XL data in the 12-lead format, ECGFounder gave the best unsupervised SR-versus-AF separation. In one 9:1 imbalanced subsample the hand-crafted features exceeded it, and under the simulated class-proportion skew the two came close. Raw flattening, which reproduces the original project's representation, and the conv-AE scored at chance on PTB-XL with all five clustering algorithms, which agrees with the original project's conclusion that the difficulty lies in the representation. On MIT-BIH the raw beat window and the conv-AE reached an ARI of at most 0.127, although the conv-AE reached a supervised ceiling of 0.927 there. The conv-AE thus carried the beat label without clustering by it, as HuBERT-ECG did for the rhythm label on PTB-XL. The study makes no comparison of the foundation models on other lead sets, because we did not apply them to the two-lead MIT-BIH beats.

The hand-crafted features are also the lowest-dimensional representation (16 values against 768 and 1024, Table~\ref{tab:repr}) and are computed from one lead without a neural network. Both foundation models need a forward pass through a network of 76 to 93 million parameters. We did not measure run time or memory, so these properties indicate cost without quantifying it. We judge that the low dimension may also suit distance-based clustering, which we did not test.

The contribution of this study is the evaluation protocol set out in the Introduction, and its parts build on existing work. The gap between linear-probe accuracy and clustering quality has been reported for self-supervised image models \cite{sslclustering2025}, the centroid averaging follows federated averaging \cite{fedavg2017}, and the representations are existing tools. The protocol reads chance-corrected clustering scores against the cluster sizes and against a patient-grouped supervised ceiling, and it sweeps the conditioner. It compares a hand-crafted baseline with two foundation models on PTB-XL, and with learned and raw representations on a MIT-BIH beat task, and adds two stress tests. Recent benchmarks evaluate foundation models on supervised downstream tasks \cite{Lunelli_2026,arxiv_2509_25095,Xu_2025} or compare their embeddings with one another \cite{lookingbeyond2026}. The protocol here adds a label-free reading against a hand-crafted baseline.

Every foundation-model statement here holds for HuBERT-ECG and ECGFounder. Other ECG foundation models with public weights include ST-MEM \cite{arxiv_2402_09450}, ECG-JEPA \cite{Kim_2027}, MERL \cite{arxiv_2403_06659}, ECGFM-KED \cite{Tian_2024,arxiv_2509_25095}, ECG-FM \cite{McKeen_2025} and ECG-CPC \cite{Lunelli_2026}. Recent benchmarks evaluate them with their public weights \cite{arxiv_2509_25095,Lunelli_2026}. Their training objectives differ, from masked reconstruction and joint-embedding prediction to contrastive alignment of signal and report text \cite{arxiv_2509_25095}. ECGFounder was included for the alignment of its embedding with the clinical label (Section~\ref{sec:repr}), so the hand-crafted features were compared with a model selected for that property, and a model trained with another objective could rank differently.

\section{Limitations}\label{sec:limitations}

ECGFounder expects 500\,Hz input. Because the 500\,Hz records could not be obtained in bulk from PhysioNet, we embedded the 100\,Hz cohort upsampled to the expected length. A consistency check on a 300-record subset, rather than the full cohort, compared native 500\,Hz with upsampled 100\,Hz input, with one $k$-means fit for each. ECGFounder's ARI was 0.896 with the native input and 0.909 with the upsampled input, so the upsampled input scored 0.013 higher. Upsampling adds no information to the 100\,Hz record, but the check shows that the change of input can still shift the clustering score. A shift of this size is small beside ECGFounder's lead of 0.242 in mean $k$-means ARI over the hand-crafted features, although the two numbers come from different sets of records. We also did not filter the foundation-model inputs as their pretraining data were filtered, and we did not rescale the HuBERT-ECG input. HuBERT-ECG was pretrained on 5\,s inputs band-pass filtered to 0.05 to 47\,Hz and rescaled to $[-1, 1]$ \cite{hubertecg2024}, and ECGFounder on filtered inputs z-scored per segment \cite{arxiv_2410_04133}. We did not test the effect of matching this preprocessing.

The foundation-model results cover two models with different pretraining. Other public models (Section~\ref{sec:discussion}) are not included, among them ECG-FM, whose embedding the closest benchmark found aligned with the clinical label \cite{lookingbeyond2026}. Both tasks are binary, and the study makes no claim about multi-class rhythm structure. Public 12-lead databases label rhythms other than SR and AF \cite{Zheng_2020,Perez_Alday_2020,ptbxl2020}. Among such rhythms, RR irregularity alone would not single out atrial fibrillation, since rhythms other than atrial fibrillation can also show irregular RR intervals \cite{clifford2017af}. The AF class itself pools atrial fibrillation with atrial flutter, which the guideline criterion for atrial fibrillation does not define. The imbalance analysis rests on one random subsample clustered once, so its result needs replication on further subsamples. The federated study simulates class-proportion skew on a single dataset. It fits the conditioner and draws the initial centroids on the pooled records, which a real multi-site deployment could not do.

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}

For the two binary tasks studied, SR versus AF on PTB-XL and N versus V on MIT-BIH, the representation changed the clustering result more than the choice among the five clustering algorithms tested. Chance-corrected external metrics were needed to see this difference. On balanced PTB-XL data ECGFounder led. In one 9:1 imbalanced subsample the 16 hand-crafted features exceeded it, and under simulated class-proportion skew they came close (0.591 against 0.612). Raw flattening and the conv-AE scored at chance on PTB-XL with every clustering algorithm tested. The silhouette and the choice of conditioner changed the apparent result, so both should be reported beside the chance-corrected scores. The foundation-model results hold for HuBERT-ECG and ECGFounder alone, and the federated results for a simulation on one dataset.

\section*{Code and data availability}
PTB-XL (version 1.0.3) \cite{ptbxl2020,ptbxl103} and the MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database (version 1.0.0) \cite{mitbih2001} are public on PhysioNet. The benchmark code, with the exact cohort definitions, the settings of Table~\ref{tab:settings} and the list of software dependencies with their minimum versions, is available at \url{https://github.com/TheAxeC/ecg-representation-clustering}. The pretrained weights of HuBERT-ECG were obtained from \url{https://huggingface.co/Edoardo-BS/hubert-ecg-base} and those of ECGFounder from \url{https://huggingface.co/PKUDigitalHealth/ECGFounder}.

\section*{Ethics}
This study uses only de-identified, publicly available databases and required no new
human-subjects data collection.

